      "title": "Yuri Gagarin: First Human in Space (1961)",
      "desc": "Vostok 1 carried Yuri Gagarin around Earth and became a major Soviet space-program triumph.",
      "content": "<p class=\"mb-4\">On 12 April 1961, Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin launched aboard Vostok 1 from Baikonur Cosmodrome. During the 108-minute flight, he completed one orbit around Earth at an altitude of about 327 kilometers. Gagarin became the first human to travel into outer space.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">The flight was a risky technical achievement. Vostok’s control system was largely automatic, and Gagarin received a code to enable manual control if needed. He ejected from the capsule during descent and landed by parachute, a fact long kept quiet because international flight standards expected pilots to land with their craft.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">Gagarin’s success gave the Soviet Union a symbolic advantage in the Space Race and inspired space programs worldwide. He became a global figure and visited many countries. He died in a training-flight crash in 1968, while 12 April is now International Day of Human Space Flight.</p>",
